{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Correction to the coordinator's correction: `--caused-by` IS live\n\nThe coordinator flagged that `rebar transition` has no `--caused-by` flag, and filed\n`brokendown-moonlit-kiskadee` (`0276-7eea-baf0-48e3`, P1) on that basis. I tested it before\nacting on it. **The flag works.** The P1 should be re-scoped.\n\n### Direct evidence — the global `rebar` binary, blame structurally unable to interfere\n\nFresh temp store, `git init` with **zero commits** and no `file_impact`, so\n`blame.derive_caused_by` cannot return anything:\n\n```\n$ rebar transition <bug> in_progress closed --class=regression --caused-by=<culprit>\ntransitioned 323c-a36f-ab61-4068: in_progress -> closed; unblocked: none\n\n$ rebar show <bug>   # deps\n[{\"target_id\": \"8aef-feb0-07e6-46c1\", \"relation\": \"caused_by\",\n  \"link_uuid\": \"d62f4e21-01a7-4c39-af92-103ae23dbcac\"}]\n```\n\nThe edge points at the culprit named on the flag. With no commits in the repo there is no\nother mechanism that could have produced it. `_parse_flags` registers it explicitly\n(`parser.add_argument(\"--caused-by\", dest=\"caused_by\", default=\"\")`,\n`src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:163`), and the pre-existing interface test\n`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_caused_by_autoderive.py::test_explicit_caused_by_on_bug_close`\ndrives the flag through the CLI and passes.\n\n### Why the original experiment misled\n\nIt used a **no-op** transition — `transition major-specified-bream open open` on an\nalready-open ticket. That returns `no change: … already open` at exit 0 from an early return,\nbefore any close-path flag handling runs. And `_parse_flags` documents that unknown tokens are\n**silently skipped by design**. So the experiment established only that an unknown flag is\nignored on a no-op transition; it never exercised a close, which is the only path where\n`--caused-by` means anything.\n\n### The half of the finding that IS real\n\n`--caused-by` is genuinely absent from `rebar transition --help` (0 occurrences), while it IS\ndocumented in `docs/user-guide.md:194`, `docs/clone-guidance.md`, and the `/rebar-debug` skill.\nThat is a real help-text gap and worth fixing — but it is a discoverability bug, not an inert\nflag, so P1 looks too high once re-scoped.\n\n### Consequences for this ticket\n\n- **The original premise stands.** Duplicates were flag-plus-existing-link, as filed. The\n  three reproductions are sound as recorded.\n- **The fix is unchanged**, and would have been correct under either premise, since\n  `_apply_caused_by` is where both the explicit culprit and the blame-derived one land.\n- **AC3 is reachable from the CLI**, not library-only. My tests drive the library seam\n  (`rebar.transition(..., caused_by=...)`), which is the same `_apply_caused_by` entry point;\n  the CLI path over it is covered by the pre-existing interface test above."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787270705655476001,"uuid":"0c44c6b5-ca2b-4f36-a341-7120da73020e"}